_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 15
---
order: 15
---
short_label: V.15
---
statement: Parts have the same ratio as their equimultiples.
---
proof:

Let *AB* be the same multiple of *C* that *DE* is of *F.*

I say that *C* is to *F* as *AB* is to *DE.*

Since *AB* is the same multiple of *C* that *DE* is of *F,* as many magnitudes as there are in *AB* equal to *C,* there are also in *DE* equal to *F.*

Divide *AB* into the magnitudes *AG, GH,* and *HB* equal to *C,* and divide *DE* into the magnitudes *DK, KL,* and *LE* equal to *F.* Then the number of the magnitudes *AG, GH,* and *HB* equals the number of the magnitudes *DK, KL,* and *LE.*

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="380" height="150" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="V.15 diagram" src="propV15.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "V.15",
        elements: [
            "U0;point;free;-1000,30;0;0",
            "U1;point;free;1000,30;0;0",
            "V0;point;free;-1000,60;0;0",
            "V1;point;free;1000,60;0;0",
            "W0;point;free;-1000,90;0;0",
            "W1;point;free;1000,90;0;0",
            "X0;point;free;-1000,120;0;0",
            "X1;point;free;1000,120;0;0",
            "C0;point;lineSlider;W0,W1,20,90;0",
            "C1;point;lineSlider;W0,W1,70,90;0",
            "C;line;connect;C0,C1;black;0;green",
            "F0;point;lineSlider;X0,X1,280,120;0",
            "F1;point;lineSlider;X0,X1,350,120;0",
            "F;line;connect;F0,F1;black;0;green",
            "A;point;lineSlider;U0,U1,20,30",
            "G;point;cutoff;A,U1,C0,C1",
            "H;point;cutoff;G,U1,C0,C1",
            "B;point;cutoff;H,U1,C0,C1",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B;0;0;green",
            "E;point;lineSlider;V0,V1,350,60",
            "L;point;cutoff;E,V0,F0,F1",
            "K;point;cutoff;L,V0,F0,F1",
            "D;point;cutoff;K,V0,F0,F1",
            "DE;line;connect;D,E;0;0;green"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

And, since *AG, GH,* and *HB* equal one another, and *DK, KL,* and *LE* also equal one another, therefore *AG* is to *DK* as *GH* is to *KL,* and as *HB* is to *LE.* [!just V.7]

Therefore one of the antecedents is to one of the consequents as the sum of the antecedents is to the sum of the consequents. Therefore *AG* is to *DK* as *AB* is to *DE.*

But *AG* equals *C* and *DK* equals *F,* therefore *C* is to *F* as *AB* is to *DE.* [!just V.12]

Therefore, *parts have the same ratio as their equimultiples.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
---
body:

This proposition states that if *n* is any number and *c* and *f* any magnitudes of the same kind, then *c*&nbsp;:&nbsp;*f*&nbsp;=&nbsp;*nc*&nbsp;:&nbsp;*nf.*

This proposition is used in the next one and a few others in Books V, VI, and XIII.
